/**
@mainpage Cortex

@section intro Introduction
Cortex makes C++ behave in the browser: stackful fibers for C++20 that share
the main thread fairly, sleep, cancel, and await JavaScript promises, so heavy
C++ compiled to WebAssembly never freezes the page. The same code runs
natively (Boost.Context) and in WebAssembly (Emscripten).

@section highlights Highlights
- @ref cortex::tiny_fiber "tiny_fiber": fibers, futures, promises, channels,
  timers, cancellation and cooperative sync primitives on one thread
- @ref cortex::tiny_fiber::CheckPoint "CheckPoint()": time-slicing for hot
  loops, a few ns when no yield is due and a no-op outside fibers
- @ref cortex::web::Await "cortex::web::Await": a fiber awaits a JavaScript
  promise while the page keeps running
- No silent failures: unobserved exceptions are rethrown, deadlocks are
  reported with the stuck fibers, stack overflows hit a guard page
- Low-level @ref cortex::Coroutine "Coroutine" and
  @ref cortex::Generator "Generator" primitives underneath

@section tinyfiber tiny_fiber
@code
#include <cortex/tiny_fiber/tiny_fiber.hpp>
namespace tf = cortex::tiny_fiber;

int answer = tf::Scheduler::Run([] {
    auto future = tf::Spawn([] {
        tf::SleepFor(std::chrono::milliseconds(10));
        return 42;
    });
    return future.Get(); // suspends this fiber, not the thread
});
@endcode

In the browser, create the scheduler once and let @c js/cortex.mjs drive it
from the event loop in time-boxed slices:
@code
std::unique_ptr<tf::Scheduler> g_scheduler;

extern "C" EMSCRIPTEN_KEEPALIVE void* start() {
    g_scheduler = tf::Scheduler::Create([] { RunMyApp(); });
    return g_scheduler.get();
}
@endcode
@code{.js}
import { drive } from "./cortex.mjs";
await drive(Module, Module._start(), { budgetMs: 8 }).done;
@endcode

@section generator Generator
@code
#include <cortex/generator.hpp>

auto numbers = cortex::Generator<int>::Make([](auto& yield) {
    yield(1);
    yield(2);
    yield(3);
});

for (int value : numbers) {
    // use value
}
@endcode

@section structure API Overview
- @ref cortex::tiny_fiber::Scheduler "Scheduler", @ref cortex::tiny_fiber::Future "Future",
  @ref cortex::tiny_fiber::Promise "Promise", @ref cortex::tiny_fiber::Channel "Channel",
  @ref cortex::tiny_fiber::Mutex "Mutex", @ref cortex::tiny_fiber::ConditionVariable "ConditionVariable"
- @ref cortex::Coroutine "Coroutine": low-level stackful coroutine
- @ref cortex::BaseCoroutine "BaseCoroutine": OO wrapper for coroutines
- @ref cortex::Generator "Generator": yield values across resumes (an input range)
- @ref cortex::PooledMemoryResource "PooledMemoryResource",
  @ref cortex::MakeGuardedStackResource "MakeGuardedStackResource": stack allocation

@section resources Resources
- <a href="https://github.com/GhevondW/cortex/blob/main/docs/guide.md">tiny_fiber guide</a>:
  every feature, with examples, and the reference for the JavaScript driver
  (also under "Related Pages" here)
- <a href="https://github.com/GhevondW/cortex/blob/main/docs/porting-guide.md">Porting guide</a>:
  un-freezing an existing Emscripten app
- <a href="https://github.com/GhevondW/cortex/blob/main/examples/linux/runtime_tour.cpp">examples/linux/runtime_tour.cpp</a>:
  every feature, natively, in one runnable file
- Live demos: https://GhevondW.github.io/cortex/ (start with
  <a href="https://GhevondW.github.io/cortex/examples/responsive_demo.html">Responsive C++</a>)
- API docs: https://GhevondW.github.io/cortex/docs/
*/
